      "id": 1,
      "claim": "The Qur'an explicitly names 25 individuals as prophets.",
      "evidence": "Qur'anic verses (e.g., 2:136, 3:84, 6:84, 7:35, 10:47, 11:25, 16:2, 21:25, 25:35, 27:15) list 25 names.",
      "weight": 0.12,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Solomon (Sulayman) is one of those 25 prophets.",
      "evidence": "Surah Al‑Anbiya 21:78‑79 and Surah An‑Naml 27:15‑16 refer to Solomon as a prophet.",
      "weight": 0.13,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Solomon therefore represents 1 ÷ 25 ≈ 4 % of the named Islamic prophets.",
      "evidence": "Simple proportion: 1 prophet (Solomon) out of 25 named prophets.",
      "weight": 0.13,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The Kingdom of Judah, after the split of the united monarchy, had 20 distinct kings (Rehoboam to Zedekiah).",
      "evidence": "Biblical lists in 1 Kings, 2 Kings, 1 Chronicles enumerate 20 Judahite monarchs.",
      "weight": 0.13,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Solomon was not a king of Judah because he ruled the united Kingdom of Israel, which preceded the Judah‑only period.",
      "evidence": "Historical chronology: Solomon reigned over the united kingdom (c. 970‑931 BCE); the Judah monarchy began with his son Rehoboam.",
      "weight": 0.14,
      "depends_on": [4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Thus Solomon’s share among the Kings of Judah is 0 ÷ 20 = 0 %.",
      "evidence": "Since Solomon is not counted among the 20 Judahite kings, his count is zero.",
      "weight": 0.13,
      "depends_on": [4, 5]
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "4 % (prophets) is greater than 0 % (Judah kings), so Solomon makes up a larger percentage of Islamic prophets than of Judah’s kings.",
      "evidence": "Direct numerical comparison of the percentages from steps 3 and 6.",
      "weight": 0.22,
      "depends_on": [3, 6]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Yes",
  "confidence": 0.96,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "No",
      "reason_rejected": "Counting Solomon among the Kings of Judah inflates the Judah denominator, but historically the term \"Kings of Judah\" refers to the post‑split monarchs (Rehoboam onward). Solomon ruled the united kingdom, so he is not part of that list; therefore his proportion among Judah kings is zero, not 1/21."
